<body>
    <main>
        <h1>LeetCode 3665: Twisted Mirror Path Count (C++)</h1>
        <div class="problem-description">
            <p><strong>Problem:</strong>
                You are given an m x n binary grid, where 0 represents an empty cell and 1 represents a
                mirror. A robot starts at the top-left cell (0, 0) and must reach the bottom-right cell
                (m - 1, n - 1), moving only right or down. When the robot tries to enter a mirror, it is
                reflected before entering: moving right into a mirror turns it down, and moving down into a
                mirror turns it right. If a reflection would take the robot outside the grid, the path is
                invalid. Return the number of unique valid paths from (0, 0) to (m - 1, n - 1), modulo
                10^9 + 7.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong>
                Input: grid = [[0,1,0],[0,0,1],[1,0,0]]<br>
                Output: 5<br>
                Explanation: There are five valid paths, such as (0,0) → (0,1) [mirror] → (1,1) → (1,2)
                [mirror] → (2,2) and (0,0) → (1,0) → (2,0) [mirror] → (2,1) → (2,2).
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="code-container">
            <button class="copy-button" onclick="copyCode(this)">Copy Code</button>
            <pre><code class="language-cpp">class Solution {
    static constexpr int mat[2][2][2] = {{{1, 1}, {1, 1}}, {{0, 1}, {1, 0}}};
    static constexpr int MOD = 1000000007;

public:
    int uniquePaths(auto&amp; grid) {
        int cols = grid[0].size(), left = 0;
        vector&lt;int&gt; dp(cols, 0);

        dp[0] = 1;

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; grid.size(); i++) {
            left = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; cols; j++) {
                int cell = grid[i][j], top = dp[j];

                dp[j] = (left * mat[cell][1][0] + top * mat[cell][1][1]) % MOD;
                left = (left * mat[cell][0][0] + top * mat[cell][0][1]) % MOD;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
};</code></pre>
